  "summary": "A traditional building style of the Dogon people in the Bandiagara Escarpment of Mali.",
  "description": "Characterized by mudbrick structures with flat roofs, granaries raised on stilts, and integrated community spaces. Developed as defensive architecture while maintaining cultural traditions and adapting to the cliffside environment.",
  "culturalContext": "Dogon architecture — Bandiagara Escarpment, Mali (UNESCO) — climbs cliffs in granary-and-gnawa compounds: toguna men's meeting shelters on thick millet-stalk columns, granaries with pottery-sealed lids, hogon chief's houses, and cliff-cave burials connecting to Tellem predecessor structures. The plan encodes cosmology (Nommo twins, Nummo spiral), and the granary facades — doors, ladders, coffered lids — became the most collected African sculptural architecture, with Amahyé masks and dama ceremonies animating the compounds.",
